{
  "teamId": 133,
  "year": 2022,
  "narrative": "The 2022 Oakland Athletics were a team in freefall, a franchise stripped to its studs as ownership tensions and a looming relocation threat hung over every game. Finishing 60-102, they scored 568 runs against a staff that surrendered 770, and their .627 OPS was among the worst in the majors. Yet even in a season this bleak, there were flickers — moments when a Seth Brown grand slam or a Shea Langeliers double reminded the sparse Coliseum crowds what baseball could feel like.\n\nApril offered a reasonably competitive 10-11 record, but the high-leverage moments told a darker story: Lou Trivino's bullpen struggles and late-inning collapses set the template for what was to come. May was a disaster at 10-21, the worst month of the season, punctuated by the cruel irony of Luis Barrera's three-run homer — a rare offensive explosion — coming in a losing cause, while the bullpen repeatedly surrendered leads. The team's top pitching asset, Frankie Montas, was being shopped to contenders even as he took the mound.\n\nJuly was the one true bright spot, a 14-12 record that briefly allowed fans to exhale. But it was a mirage built on schedule variance rather than genuine improvement, and August and September brought the inevitable collapse — Sam Moll surrendering a Kyle Tucker grand slam, Norge Ruiz giving up a three-run Joey Meneses bomb, the losses piling up in humiliating fashion. The September surge by young players like Shea Langeliers and Vimael Machín offered tantalizing hints of a future that wouldn't be built in Oakland.\n\nThe 2022 A's were ultimately a eulogy dressed in green and gold — a 102-loss team caught between a past of Moneyball glory and an uncertain future, their most memorable moments belonging largely to the opposition. The sell-off of Montas, Stephen Vogt's farewell home run off his own team's arm, and a final-week loss to Seattle underscored a season that was less a competitive campaign than a long goodbye to a franchise slowly slipping away from its city.",
